  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 100 AM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nWell Above Normal Temperatures Wednesday:\n\nPersistently variable and low confidence pattern through the rest of \nthe week. Initial low confidence precipitation chances continue \ngrazing locally western counties from southeast Minnesota into \ncentral Wisconsin overnight through Tuesday night, with a band of \nlighter showers potentially poking northeast and grazing locally \nsouthern peripheral counties from northeast Iowa into southwest \nWisconsin.\n\nA tightening low level perturbation from the Rockies through the \ncentral CONUS provides enhanced low level warm air advection \nWednesday, likely resulting in relatively weak warm temperature \nrecords being broken.\n\nWednesday Night Rainfall:\n\nSubsequent impact from accompanying increased low level moisture \nwill be widespread preciptiation chances across the forecast area. \nConfidence in available forcing as the front continues shifting east \nremains the inherent forecast challenge. Most recent (27.18Z) LREF \n(GEFS/EPS/GEPS) have increased spatial coverage for 100% \nprobabilities of 0.1\" of rainfall in 24 hours, primarily Wednesday \nnight. Timing confidence initially in the 7PM-10PM window along \nwestern counties, shifting east into the overnight hours.\n\nLow Confidence In A Wet Weekend:\n\nSubsequent rainfall potential Friday night and through the weekend \nhas increased/decreased probabilities for 0.1\" of rainfall in most \nrecent GEFS/EPS compared to their respective previous runs. Although \nintra-LREF member confidence varies, inter-model agreement has \nincreased in most recent runs on synoptic pattern with influence \nfrom the southeastern U.S. synoptic anticyclone mostly shunted \nsoutheast of the forecast area, permitting low level moisture \nadvection to penetrate towards the forecast area as it lifts through \nmuch of the central CONUS, as eventual cyclogenesis advects east \nacross the Missouri and Mississippi River Valleys. Location of the \nlow center and accompanying cyclogenesis will determine local \nweather impacts through the weekend.",
